---
description: Use for Vue single-file components, layouts, pages, or composables. Do not use for server-only TypeScript with no Vue reactivity or template.
globs:
  - "app/components/**/*.vue"
  - "app/layouts/**/*.vue"
  - "app/pages/**/*.vue"
  - "app/composables/**/*.ts"
alwaysApply: false
---

- Use `<script setup lang="ts">` with typed `defineProps<{ ... }>()` and
  `defineEmits<{ ... }>()`; do not fall back to the runtime prop-object form or
  the Options API in this Composition-API-only codebase.
- Keep data flow one-way: props down, events up. Never mutate a prop inside the
  component that receives it; the props/emits shape is the component's public
  contract.
- Prefer `computed` for a value derived from other reactive state; reserve
  `watch`/`watchEffect` for an actual side effect (fetch, DOM measurement,
  external subscription). A `watch` that only recomputes a value is a signal to
  use `computed` instead.
- Keep templates declarative: extract a non-trivial expression, multi-step
  transformation, or repeated inline condition into a `computed` or a named
  method rather than writing it inline in the template.
- Never destructure a reactive object or the `props` object directly; reactivity
  is lost silently. Use `toRefs`/`toRef`, or keep the whole reference and access
  fields through it. This is a common source of a bug that a non-programmer
  reviewer cannot spot by reading the rendered page.
- Give every `v-for` a stable, identity-based `:key`; never key on the array
  index when items can be reordered, inserted, or removed.
- Name composables `use<Noun>`, keep one clear responsibility per composable,
  and touch the DOM or browser APIs only inside a lifecycle hook
  (`onMounted`, `onUnmounted`) so the composable stays SSR-safe. Mirror
  `useSceneLifecycle.ts` for the isolation pattern used by 3D scenes.
- Keep component-local UI state local; lift it to a composable or parent only
  when a second independent consumer needs the same state (same threshold as
  `.cursor/rules/architecture-refactoring.mdc`).
- Use `ref()` for a primitive value and `reactive()` only for a genuine object
  whose properties are read together; do not mix the two for the same piece of
  state.
- Reuse an existing design token from `app/assets/styles/tokens.css`, a native
  CSS feature, or a Reka UI primitive before adding component-scoped styling
  that hardcodes a colour, spacing, or breakpoint value already expressed as a
  token.
